window.docContents[127].resolve({"/Poly/#Logical-Foundations--Poly___-Polymorphism-and-Higher-Order-Functions--Functions-as-Data--Fold":{"contents":"An even more powerful higher-order function is\nfold. It is the inspiration for the \"reduce\"\noperation that lies at the heart of Google's map/reduce\ndistributed programming framework.\n\ndef fold {α : Type} {β : Type} (f : α → β → β) (l : List α) (b : β) : β :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => b\n  | head :: tail => f head (fold f tail b)\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIntuitively, the behavior of the fold operation is to\ninsert a given binary operator f between every pair of elements\nin a given list. For example, fold (· + ·) [1, 2, 3, 4]\nintuitively means 1 + 2 + 3 + 4. To make this precise, we also\nneed a \"starting element\" that serves as the initial second input\nto f. So, for example,fold (· + ·) [1, 2, 3, 4] 0\nyields1 + (2 + (3 + (4 + 0))).\n\n\nexample : fold (· && ·) [true, true, false, true] true = false := by rfl\n\nexample : fold (· * ·) [1, 2, 3, 4] 1 = 24 := by rfl\n\nexample : fold (· ++ ·) [[1], [], [2, 3], [4]] [] = [1, 2, 3, 4] := by rfl\n\nexample : fold (fun l n => l.length + n) [[1], [], [2, 3, 2], [4]] 0 = 5 := by rfl\n\ntheorem fold_nil {α : Type} {β : Type} {f : α → β → β} {b : β} : fold f [] b = b := by rfl\n\ntheorem fold_cons {α : Type} {β : Type} {f : α → β → β} {head : α} {tail : List α} {b : β} :\n    fold f (head :: tail) b = f head (fold f tail b) := by rfl\n\n\nHere is the definition of fold again:def fold {α : Type} {β : Type} (f : α → β → β) (l : List α) (b : β) : β :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => b\n  | head :: tail => f head (fold f tail b)\nWhat is the type of @fold?(A) {α β : Type} → (α → β → β) → List α → β → β(B) α → β → (α → β → β) → List α → β → β(C) {α β : Type} → α → β → β → List α → β → β(D) α → β → α → β → β → List α → β → β\n\nWhat does fold (· + ·) [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 simplify to?(A) [1, 2, 3, 4](B) 0(C) 10(D) [3, 7, 0]\n\nObserve that the type of fold is parameterized by two type\nvariables, α and β, and the parameter f is a binary operator\nthat takes an α and a β and returns a β.\nThe examples above show one instance where it is useful for α\nand β to be different. Can you think of any others?There are many. For example, we could use fold to count the\nnumber of true elements in a list of booleans. Here α would\nbe Bool and β would be Nat.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Poly: Polymorphism and Higher-Order Functions\u0009Functions as Data","header":"6.2.5. Fold","id":"/Poly/#Logical-Foundations--Poly___-Polymorphism-and-Higher-Order-Functions--Functions-as-Data--Fold"},"/Preface/#Logical-Foundations--Preface--Practicalities--Recommended-Citation-Format":{"contents":"If you want to refer to this volume in your own writing, please\ndo so as follows:\n\nFix.  Give a citation for the whole series also?\n\n@book            {$FIRSTAUTHOR:SF$VOLUMENUMBER,\nauthor       =   {$AUTHORS},\neditor       =   {Benjamin C. Pierce},\ntitle        =   \"$VOLUMENAME\",\nseries       =   \"Software Foundations\",\nvolume       =   \"$VOLUMENUMBER\",\nyear         =   \"$VOLUMEYEAR\",\npublisher    =   \"Electronic textbook\",\nnote         =   {Version $VERSION, \\URL<http://softwarefoundations.cis.upenn.edu>}\n}\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Preface\u0009Practicalities","header":"1.3.3. Recommended Citation Format","id":"/Preface/#Logical-Foundations--Preface--Practicalities--Recommended-Citation-Format"}});